     below. Keeping colour out of this rule is deliberate — when it lived
     here it outranked global.css's `.on-dark .eyebrow { color: gold }` on
     specificity, which rendered the hero's "1922 — Today" royal-on-navy at
     2.01:1 (invisible). Same trap produced the earlier invisible
     `feature-title--light` heading on the navy band. */
  /* The eyebrow typography override that used to live here is gone: these
     exact values (Montserrat 700, 11px, 0.15em, uppercase) are now the global
     `.eyebrow` rule, so all 27 pages match the type decision in tokens.css
     instead of just this one. */
  /* Colour is deliberately NOT set here. global.css now pairs
     `.eyebrow { color: var(--accent-text) }` with an `.on-dark` override, which
     gives every page the accessible pairing. A `body.page-home .eyebrow`
     colour rule here would be specificity 0,2,1 and would silently outrank
     that `.on-dark` override (0,2,0) — the exact trap that once rendered the
     hero eyebrow royal-on-navy at 2:1. */

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  :global(body.page-home .hero__title) {
